{
  "mn18:0.1": "Middle Discourses 18 ",
  "mn18:0.2": "The Honey-Cake ",
  "mn18:1.1": "So I have heard. ",
  "mn18:1.2": "At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Sakyans, near Kapilavatthu in the Banyan Tree Monastery. ",
  "mn18:2.1": "Then the Buddha robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, entered Kapilavatthu for alms. ",
  "mn18:2.2": "He wandered for alms in Kapilavatthu. After the meal, on his return from almsround, he went to the Great Wood for the day’s meditation, ",
  "mn18:2.3": "plunged deep into it, and sat at the root of a young wood-apple tree to meditate. ",
  "mn18:3.1": "Daṇḍapāṇi the Sakyan, while going for a walk, ",
  "mn18:3.2": "plunged deep into the Great Wood. He approached the Buddha and exchanged greetings with him. ",
  "mn18:3.3": "When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he stood to one side leaning on his staff, and said to the Buddha, ",
  "mn18:3.4": "“What is the ascetic’s doctrine? What does he assert?” ",
  "mn18:4.1": "“Respectable sir, my doctrine is such that one does not argue with anyone in this world with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans. And it is such that perceptions do not underlie the brahmin who lives detached from sensual pleasures, without indecision, stripped of worry, and rid of craving for rebirth in this or that state. ",
  "mn18:4.2": "That is my doctrine, and that is what I assert.” ",
